<hr />
<p style="text-align: justify;"> </p>

<h1 data-number="1" id="Workflow Description" style="text-align: justify;">Workflow Description</h1>

<h2 data-number="1.1" id="Key Workflow Steps">Key Workflow Steps</h2>

<ul>
	<li>
	<p>Defines a Snakemake workflow module named <code>spilterlize_integrate</code> with a minimum Snakemake version requirement, report generation, and project configuration loading.</p>
	</li>
	<li>
	<p>Loads sample annotation data and derives analysis splits from configured annotation columns and their unique values, alongside a global result directory structure.</p>
	</li>
	<li>
	<p>Constructs collections of normalization, integration, and transformed data representations based on configuration parameters, including optional batch-effect correction and highly variable feature outputs.</p>
	</li>
	<li>
	<p>Sets final workflow targets around visualization outputs for multiple data representations across all splits, along with exported environment files and copied configuration and annotation records.</p>
	</li>
	<li>
	<p>Organizes the workflow through included rule files covering processing, normalization, integration, visualization, and environment export.</p>
	</li>
</ul>

<h2 data-number="1.2" id="Workflow Overview">Workflow Overview</h2>

<p>This workflow supports exploratory preprocessing and comparison of count-based datasets across biologically or technically defined sample subsets. Based on a sample annotation table, it can generate analyses for the full dataset and for configured splits, then produce visual outputs for raw counts, filtered data, multiple normalization variants, optional batch-effect–integrated data, and highly variable feature selections. It is useful in projects that need to assess how normalization and integration choices affect downstream structure and clustering across different sample groups. Researchers working with annotated count matrices, especially those comparing preprocessing strategies or examining batch-corrected versus uncorrected representations, would benefit from this workflow’s organized production of summary plots, clustered heatmaps, and associated exported configurations and environments.</p>

<h1 data-number="2" id="Rule Overview">Rule Overview</h1>

<h2 data-number="2.1" id="Rule Overview">Rule Overview</h2>

<hr />
<ul>
	<li>
	<p><strong>Purpose</strong>: Final target rule that collects the required plot files, exported environment files, and copied configuration/annotation files.</p>
	</li>
	<li>
	<p><strong>Input</strong>:</p>

	<pre>
<code>expand(os.path.join(result_path, '\{split}','plots','\{all_data}.png'), split=splits, all_data=all_data)
expand(os.path.join(result_path, '\{split}','plots','\{all_data}_heatmap_clustered.png'), split=splits, all_data=all_data)
expand(os.path.join(result_path,'\{split}','plots','CFA.png'), split=splits)
expand(os.path.join(result_path,'envs','\{env}.yaml'),env=["python_basics","edgeR","cqn","ggplot"])
os.path.join(result_path,'configs','\{}_config.yaml'.format(config["project_name"]))
os.path.join(result_path,'configs','\{}_annot.csv'.format(config["project_name"]))</code></pre>
	</li>
	<li>
	<p><strong>Output</strong>:</p>

	<pre>
<code>-</code></pre>
	</li>
	<li>
	<p><strong>Dependencies</strong>:</p>

	<ul>
		<li>
		<p>env_yaml</p>
		</li>
		<li>
		<p>copy_config_file</p>
		</li>
		<li>
		<p>copy_annot_file</p>
		</li>
	</ul>
	</li>
</ul>

<hr />
<ul>
	<li>
	<p><strong>External Rules</strong>:</p>

	<ul>
		<li>
		<p>process.smk</p>
		</li>
		<li>
		<p>normalize.smk</p>
		</li>
		<li>
		<p>integrate.smk</p>
		</li>
		<li>
		<p>visualize.smk</p>
		</li>
		<li>
		<p>envs_export.smk</p>
		</li>
	</ul>
	</li>
</ul>

<h1 data-number="3" id="Configuration and Environment">Configuration and Environment</h1>

<h2 data-number="3.1" id="Configuration">Configuration</h2>

<ul>
	<li>
	<p>Configuration file: <code>config/config.yaml</code></p>
	</li>
	<li>
	<p>Loaded from <code>config/config.yaml</code></p>
	</li>
</ul>

<h2 data-number="3.2" id="Config Parameters Accessed">Config parameters accessed</h2>

<ul>
	<li>
	<p><code>annotation</code>: path to the annotation table loaded with <code>pandas.read_csv(..., index_col=0)</code></p>
	</li>
	<li>
	<p><code>result_path</code>: base output directory, combined with the module name <code>spilterlize_integrate</code></p>
	</li>
	<li>
	<p><code>split_by</code>: annotation columns used to define data splits</p>
	</li>
	<li>
	<p><code>edgeR_parameters.quantification</code>: used to construct normalization method names when <code>method</code> contains <code>"none"</code></p>
	</li>
	<li>
	<p><code>edgeR_parameters.method</code>: list of edgeR normalization methods used to derive normalization outputs</p>
	</li>
	<li>
	<p><code>cqn_parameters.x</code>: determines whether <code>normCQN</code> is included</p>
	</li>
	<li>
	<p><code>voom_parameters.normalize.method</code>: determines whether <code>normVOOM</code> is included</p>
	</li>
	<li>
	<p><code>removeBatchEffect_parameters.desired</code>: determines whether integrated data outputs are generated</p>
	</li>
	<li>
	<p><code>removeBatchEffect_parameters.norm_methods</code>: normalization methods selected for integration output naming</p>
	</li>
	<li>
	<p><code>project_name</code>: used to name exported config and annotation files</p>
	</li>
	<li>
	<p><code>threads</code>: thread count for rule <code>all</code></p>
	</li>
	<li>
	<p><code>mem</code>: memory resource for rule <code>all</code></p>
	</li>
</ul>

<h2 data-number="3.3" id="Explicit Versions">Explicit versions</h2>

<ul>
	<li>
	<p>Snakemake: <code>8.20.1</code></p>
	</li>
</ul>

<h2 data-number="3.4" id="Further Reading">Further Reading</h2>

<p>For details on Snakemake configuration and workflow setup, see the official Snakemake documentation: https://snakemake.readthedocs.io/en/stable/snakefiles/configuration.html</p>
]]></question>
