<h1 id="9-operational-notes">9. Operational Notes</h1>

<h2 id="91-coordinate-format-restrictions">9.1 Coordinate format restrictions</h2>

<p>The coordinate regex only accepts chromosome strings of the form:</p>

<p>chr[0-9XY]+:[start]-[end]</p>

<p>This means it may not match:</p>

<ul>
	<li>mitochondrial contigs like chrM</li>
	<li>alternative contigs</li>
	<li>lower-case chromosome names</li>
	<li>non-human naming conventions such as 1:100-200 without chr prefix</li>
</ul>

<p>This is important for portability across genome builds and organisms.</p>

<h2 id="92-sample-metadata-tsv-assumptions">9.2 Sample metadata TSV assumptions</h2>

<p>For single-cell grouping files, the workflow assumes:</p>

<ul>
	<li>tab-separated format</li>
	<li>no header</li>
	<li>group labels stored in the second column (index 1)</li>
</ul>

<p>If the file format differs, grouping extraction will fail or produce incorrect results.</p>

<h2 id="93-missing-gene-behavior">9.3 Missing-gene behavior</h2>

<p>Unresolvable gene symbols do not stop the workflow globally. Instead, they are logged to genes_not_found.csv and excluded from downstream target generation.</p>

<p>This improves robustness for partially outdated or mixed gene lists.</p>

<h1 id="10-reproducibility-and-provenance">10. Reproducibility and Provenance</h1>

<p>The workflow supports reproducibility through:</p>

<ul>
	<li>strict minimum Snakemake version enforcement</li>
	<li>exported environment YAMLs</li>
	<li>copied config and annotation files</li>
	<li>workflow report registration via report/workflow.rst</li>
</ul>

<h1 id="11-gaps-in-the-available-source">11. Gaps in the Available Source</h1>

<p>Some technical details cannot be documented with certainty from the uploaded Snakefile alone:</p>

<ul>
	<li>the exact schema of sample_annotation beyond bam and group</li>
	<li>the full schema of gene_list beyond gene_region and ymax</li>
	<li>implementations of parse_gene() and parse_region()</li>
	<li>actual rule graph inside common.smk, envs_export.smk, prepare.smk, and visualization.smk</li>
	<li>the exact toolchain for generating bigWigs, track images, and track hubs</li>
</ul>

<h1 id="12-suggested-minimal-example-config">12. Suggested Minimal Example Config</h1>

<p>A plausible minimal config, inferred from the Snakefile, would look like:</p>

<p>sample_annotation: config/samples.csv gene_list: config/gene_list.csv result_path: results file_type: png genome: hg38 project_name: example_project mem: 4000 threads: 1</p>

<p>This is an inferred example for documentation convenience, not something explicitly provided by the source file.</p>

<h1 id="13-suggested-minimal-example-tables">13. Suggested Minimal Example Tables</h1>

<p>Sample annotation CSV</p>

<p>bam,group</p>

<p>data/sample1.bam,treated</p>

<p>data/sample2.bam,control</p>

<p>data/sc_sample.bam,metadata/sc_groups.tsv</p>

<p>Gene list CSV</p>

<p>gene_region,ymax</p>

<p>TP53,50</p>

<p>chr1:100000-120000,100</p>

<p>These examples are inferred from the code paths and column references, not copied from any provided input file.</p>

<h1 id="14-summary">14. Summary</h1>

<p>This Snakemake workflow is a modular genome-track generation pipeline that:</p>

<ul>
	<li>loads project config and annotation metadata</li>
	<li>supports both regular grouped samples and single-cell metadata-driven grouping</li>
	<li>resolves target genes or coordinate regions into genomic intervals</li>
	<li>skips unresolved genes while recording them</li>
	<li>delegates execution to included rule files</li>
	<li>produces track images, UCSC hub metadata, environment exports, and configuration snapshots</li>
</ul>
]]></question>
    <help/>
    <language><![CDATA[en]]></language>
    <script/>
