<h1>Technical Documentation: spilterlize_integrate Snakemake Workflow</h1>

<h1>1. Purpose</h1>

<ol>
</ol>

<p>The spilterlize_integrate workflow is a Snakemake-based data processing pipeline designed to:</p>

<ul>
	<li>load project configuration and sample annotation</li>
	<li>derive multiple dataset splits from annotation metadata</li>
	<li>generate normalized expression matrices using several supported methods</li>
	<li>optionally apply batch-effect correction / integration</li>
	<li>derive HVF-transformed datasets</li>
	<li>generate visualization outputs for all data representations</li>
	<li>export workflow environment and configuration artifacts for reproducibility</li>
</ul>

<p>The workflow is modular and delegates most processing to included rule files. The top-level Snakefile primarily defines:</p>

<ul>
	<li>global imports and configuration</li>
	<li>derived workflow state</li>
	<li>data categories and target expansions</li>
	<li>included rule modules</li>
</ul>

<h1>2. Workflow Metadata</h1>

<p>Module name</p>

<p>module_name = "spilterlize_integrate"</p>

<p>This name is used to construct the final output directory under the configured result_path.</p>

<p>Snakemake version requirement</p>

<p>min_version("8.20.1")</p>

<p>The workflow requires Snakemake 8.20.1 or newer.</p>

<p>Workflow report</p>

<p>report: "report/workflow.rst"</p>

<p>A report is expected at report/workflow.rst, indicating the pipeline is intended to support Snakemake reporting.</p>

<h1>3. External Dependencies</h1>

<ol start="3">
</ol>

<p>The workflow imports the following Python libraries:</p>

<ul>
	<li>yaml</li>
	<li>pandas</li>
	<li>os</li>
	<li>json</li>
	<li>csv</li>
	<li>sys</li>
	<li>subprocess</li>
</ul>

<p>It also imports Snakemake utilities:</p>

<ul>
	<li>validate</li>
	<li>min_version</li>
</ul>

<p>A commented global Conda environment is declared:</p>

<p>conda: "envs/global.yaml"</p>

<p>This suggests environment management may be handled either globally or per-rule through the included rule files.</p>

<h1>4. Configuration Inputs</h1>

<ol start="4">
</ol>

<p>Main configuration file</p>

<p>configfile: os.path.join("config","config.yaml")</p>

<p>The workflow expects a YAML configuration at:</p>

<p>config/config.yaml</p>

<p>Annotation table</p>

<p>annotation = pd.read_csv(config["annotation"], index_col=0)</p>

<p>The configuration must define an annotation path pointing to a CSV file. The first column is used as the row index.</p>

<p>The annotation table is central to split generation and likely also used downstream for normalization, integration, and plotting.</p>

<h1>5. Output Directory Layout</h1>

<ol start="5">
</ol>

<p>The root result directory is constructed as:</p>

<p>result_path = os.path.join(config["result_path"], module_name)</p>

<p>So all module outputs are written under:</p>

<p>\{config[result_path]}/spilterlize_integrate/</p>

<p>Within that root, outputs are organized by split, for example:</p>

<p>\{result_path}/\{split}/...</p>

<h1>6. Dynamic Workflow State</h1>

<ol start="6">
</ol>

<p>The workflow derives multiple lists that determine its final targets.</p>

<h2>6.1 Split generation</h2>

<p>The workflow initializes with a default split:</p>

<p>splits = ['all']</p>

<p>It then iterates over variables listed in config["split_by"]. For each variable that exists as a column in the annotation table, the workflow creates one split per unique value:</p>

<p>for split_var in config["split_by"]:</p>

<p>    if split_var in annotation.columns:</p>

<p>        splits = splits + [f'\{split_var}_\{entry}' for entry in annotation[split_var].unique()]</p>

<p>Example</p>

<p>If:</p>

<p>split_by:</p>

<ul>
	<li>condition</li>
	<li>batch</li>
</ul>

<p>and annotation contains:</p>

<ul>
	<li>condition: control, treated</li>
	<li>batch: A, B</li>
</ul>

<p>then generated splits would be:</p>

<p>all</p>

<p>condition_control</p>

<p>condition_treated</p>

<p>batch_A</p>

<p>batch_B</p>

<p>Notes</p>

<ul>
	<li>Any split_by entry not present in the annotation columns is silently ignored.</li>
	<li>The all split is always included.</li>
	<li>Unique values are taken directly from the annotation table and embedded in directory names.</li>
</ul>

<h2>6.2 Normalization methods</h2>

<p>The workflow builds normalization targets from three method families:</p>

<p>edgeR-based methods</p>

<p>norm_edgeR_methods = ["norm"+config["edgeR_parameters"]["quantification"] if i == "none" else "norm"+i for i in config["edgeR_parameters"]["method"]]</p>

<p>Behavior:</p>

<ul>
	<li>each entry in config["edgeR_parameters"]["method"] becomes norm&lt;method&gt;<method><method></method></method></li>
	<li>if a method is "none", it is replaced by:</li>
</ul>

<p>norm\{quantification}</p>

<p>    where quantification comes from config["edgeR_parameters"]["quantification"]</p>

<p>This means "none" is treated as a request to use raw quantification naming rather than a literal "normnone" output.</p>

<p>CQN</p>

<p>norm_cqn = ["normCQN"] if config["cqn_parameters"]["x"] != "" else []</p>

<p>CQN normalization is enabled only when cqn_parameters.x is a non-empty string.</p>

<p>VOOM</p>

<p>norm_voom = ["normVOOM"] if config["voom_parameters"]["normalize.method"] != "" else []</p>

<p>VOOM normalization is enabled only when voom_parameters.normalize.method is non-empty.</p>

<p>Combined normalization list</p>

<p>norm_methods = norm_edgeR_methods + norm_cqn + norm_voom</p>

<h2>6.3 Integration / batch correction targets</h2>

<p>Optional integration outputs are created only if the config contains at least one desired target:</p>

<p>if len(config["removeBatchEffect_parameters"]["desired"])!=0:</p>

<p>    integration_data = config["removeBatchEffect_parameters"]["norm_methods"]</p>

<p>    integration_data = ["norm"+method+"_integrated" for method in integration_data]</p>

<p>This implies:</p>

<ul>
	<li>integration is controlled by removeBatchEffect_parameters.desired</li>
	<li>source normalization methods for integration come from removeBatchEffect_parameters.norm_methods</li>
	<li>output naming follows:</li>
</ul>

<p>norm&lt;method&gt;<method><method>_integrated</method></method></p>

<p>Example</p>

<p>If:</p>

<p>removeBatchEffect_parameters:</p>

<p>    desired: ["batch"]</p>

<p>    norm_methods: ["CPM", "VOOM"]</p>

<p>then:</p>

<p>normCPM_integrated</p>

<p>normVOOM_integrated</p>

<h2>6.4 HVF-transformed datasets</h2>

<p>HVF outputs are defined as follows:</p>

<p>if len(integration_data)>0:</p>

<p>    transformed_data = [method+"_HVF" for method in integration_data]</p>

<p>elif len(norm_methods)>0:</p>

<p>    transformed_data = [method+"_HVF" for method in norm_methods]</p>

<p>This means:</p>

<ul>
	<li>if integration outputs exist, HVF is applied to the integrated datasets</li>
	<li>otherwise, HVF is applied directly to normalization outputs</li>
	<li>if neither exists, no HVF datasets are defined</li>
</ul>

<p>Priority rule</p>

<p>Integration-derived data has precedence over normalization-derived data for HVF selection.</p>

<h2>6.5 All visualization data categories</h2>

<p>The workflow aggregates all data classes into a master list:</p>

<p>all_data = ['counts','filtered'] + norm_methods + integration_data + transformed_data</p>

<p>This list drives plot generation.</p>

<p>The expected visualization space therefore includes:</p>

<ul>
	<li>raw counts</li>
	<li>filtered counts</li>
	<li>each normalization output</li>
	<li>each integrated output</li>
	<li>each HVF-transformed output</li>
</ul>

<h2>7. Target Rule</h2>

<p>The workflow’s final outputs are defined in rule all.</p>

<p>Active final targets</p>

<p>Currently enabled targets are:</p>

<p>Per-split plots</p>

<p>expand(os.path.join(result_path, '\{split}','plots','\{all_data}.png'), ...)</p>

<p>expand(os.path.join(result_path, '\{split}','plots','\{all_data}_heatmap_clustered.png'), ...)</p>

<p>expand(os.path.join(result_path,'\{split}','plots','CFA.png'), ...)</p>

<p>For every split and every data representation in all_data, the workflow expects:</p>

<ul>
	<li>a PNG plot:</li>
</ul>

<p>\{result_path}/\{split}/plots/\{all_data}.png</p>

<ul>
	<li>a clustered heatmap:</li>
</ul>

<p>\{result_path}/\{split}/plots/\{all_data}_heatmap_clustered.png</p>

<p>Additionally, for each split:</p>

<ul>
	<li>a CFA plot:</li>
</ul>

<p>\{result_path}/\{split}/plots/CFA.png</p>

<p>Exported environments</p>

<p>expand(os.path.join(result_path,'envs','\{env}.yaml'), env=["python_basics","edgeR","cqn","ggplot"])</p>

<p>The workflow expects YAML exports for:</p>

<ul>
	<li>python_basics</li>
	<li>edgeR</li>
	<li>cqn</li>
	<li>ggplot</li>
</ul>

<p>These likely represent frozen software environments for reproducibility.</p>

<p>Configuration copies</p>

<p>os.path.join(result_path,'configs','\{}_config.yaml'.format(config["project_name"])) os.path.join(result_path,'configs','\{}_annot.csv'.format(config["project_name"]))</p>

<p>The workflow also requires archived copies of:</p>

<ul>
	<li>the project configuration YAML</li>
	<li>the annotation CSV</li>
</ul>

<p>named using config["project_name"].</p>

<p>Commented targets</p>

<p>Several expected intermediate targets are present but commented out:</p>

<ul>
	<li>split count matrices</li>
	<li>split annotations</li>
	<li>filtered matrices</li>
	<li>normalization matrices</li>
	<li>integration matrices</li>
	<li>HVF matrices</li>
</ul>

<p>This suggests the pipeline may still generate these files through downstream rules, but they are not currently enforced as final top-level targets.</p>

<p>The active rule all focuses on:</p>

<ul>
	<li>plots</li>
	<li>environment exports</li>
	<li>copied configuration artifacts</li>
</ul>

<h1>8. Modular Rule Structure</h1>

<ol start="8">
</ol>

<p>The workflow includes the following rule files:</p>

<p>include: os.path.join("rules", "process.smk")</p>

<p>include: os.path.join("rules", "normalize.smk")</p>

<p>include: os.path.join("rules", "integrate.smk")</p>

<p>include: os.path.join("rules", "visualize.smk")</p>

<p>include: os.path.join("rules", "envs_export.smk")</p>

<p>Expected responsibilities</p>

<p>rules/process.smk</p>

<p>Likely handles:</p>

<ul>
	<li>initial count/annotation splitting</li>
	<li>filtering steps</li>
	<li>preparation of per-split input matrices</li>
</ul>

<p>rules/normalize.smk</p>

<p>Likely handles:</p>

<ul>
	<li>edgeR normalization</li>
	<li>CQN normalization</li>
	<li>VOOM normalization</li>
</ul>

<p>rules/integrate.smk</p>

<p>Likely handles:</p>

<ul>
	<li>batch-effect correction</li>
	<li>integration of normalized matrices using removeBatchEffect</li>
</ul>

<p>rules/visualize.smk</p>

<p>Likely handles:</p>

<ul>
	<li>general per-dataset plotting</li>
	<li>clustered heatmaps</li>
	<li>CFA plots</li>
</ul>

<p>rules/envs_export.smk</p>

<p>Likely handles:</p>

<ul>
	<li>exporting Conda or tool environments</li>
	<li>saving workflow configuration and annotation snapshots</li>
</ul>

<h1>9. Resource and Execution Settings</h1>

<ol start="9">
</ol>

<p>The all rule contains global defaults:</p>

<p>threads: config.get("threads", 1)</p>

<p>resources:</p>

<p>    mem_mb=config.get("mem", "16000")</p>

<p>Interpretation</p>

<ul>
	<li>default thread count is 1 unless threads is set in config</li>
	<li>default memory budget is 16000 MB unless mem is set in config</li>
</ul>

<p>A log file is also defined:</p>

<p>log:</p>

<p>    os.path.join("logs","rules","all.log")</p>

<p>So the top-level aggregation rule logs to:</p>

<p>logs/rules/all.log</p>

<h1>10. Configuration Schema Expectations</h1>

<ol start="10">
</ol>

<p>Based on the Snakefile, the following config keys are required or expected:</p>

<p>annotation: &lt;path/to/annotation.csv&gt;<path annotation.csv="" to=""></path></p>

<p>result_path: &lt;output/root&gt;</p>

<p>project_name: &lt;project identifier&gt;<project identifier=""></project></p>

<p>split_by:</p>

<ul>
	<li>&lt;annotation column 1&gt;<annotation 1="" column=""></annotation></li>
	<li>&lt;annotation column 2&gt;<annotation 2="" column=""></annotation></li>
</ul>

<p>edgeR_parameters:</p>

<p>    quantification: &lt;string&gt;<string></string></p>

<p>    method:</p>

<ul>
	<li>&lt;method1&gt;<method1></method1></li>
	<li>&lt;method2&gt;<method2></method2></li>
	<li>none</li>
</ul>

<p>cqn_parameters:</p>

<p>     x: &lt;string or empty string&gt;<string empty="" or="" string=""><br />voom_parameters:</string></p>

<p>     normalize.method: &lt;string or empty string&gt;<string empty="" or="" string=""><br />removeBatchEffect_parameters:</string></p>

<p>    desired:</p>

<ul>
	<li>&lt;batch variable(s)&gt;<batch variable=""></batch></li>
</ul>

<p>    norm_methods:</p>

<ul>
	<li>&lt;method names&gt;<method names=""></method></li>
</ul>

<p>threads: &lt;int&gt;<int><br />mem: &lt;int or string&gt;<int or="" string=""><int or="" string=""><int or="" string=""><br /> </int></int></int></int></p>

<p>Important behavioral details</p>

<ul>
	<li>split_by values must match annotation column names to have any effect.</li>
	<li>cqn_parameters.x == "" disables CQN targets.</li>
	<li>voom_parameters.normalize.method == "" disables VOOM targets.</li>
	<li>removeBatchEffect_parameters.desired must be non-empty to enable integration outputs.</li>
	<li>HVF datasets prefer integrated outputs when available.</li>
</ul>

<h1>11. Output Naming Conventions</h1>

<ol start="11">
</ol>

<p>Split-level directories</p>

<p>\{result_path}/\{split}/</p>

<p>Plot outputs</p>

<p>plots/\{dataset}.png</p>

<p>plots/\{dataset}_heatmap_clustered.png</p>

<p>plots/CFA.png</p>

<p>Dataset naming classes</p>

<ul>
	<li>counts</li>
	<li>filtered</li>
	<li>norm&lt;method&gt;<method><method></method></method></li>
	<li>norm&lt;method&gt;<method><method>_integrated</method></method></li>
	<li>norm&lt;method&gt;<method><method>_HVF</method></method></li>
	<li>norm&lt;method&gt;​​​​​​​<method><method>_integrated_HVF</method></method></li>
</ul>

<p>Depending on config-driven enablement.</p>

<h1>12. Execution Flow Summary</h1>

<ol start="12">
</ol>

<p>Conceptually, the pipeline operates in this order:</p>

<ol>
	<li>load config and annotation</li>
	<li>derive split list from annotation metadata</li>
	<li>derive enabled normalization methods</li>
	<li>derive optional integration targets</li>
	<li>derive HVF targets</li>
	<li>assemble all visualization data categories</li>
	<li>execute included processing, normalization, integration, visualization, and export rules</li>
	<li>complete when all plot and reproducibility artifacts exist for every split</li>
</ol>

<h1>13. Reproducibility Features</h1>

<ol start="13">
</ol>

<p>The workflow contains several reproducibility-oriented design elements:</p>

<ul>
	<li>explicit minimum Snakemake version</li>
	<li>structured modular rule files</li>
	<li>environment export targets</li>
	<li>copied configuration snapshot</li>
	<li>copied annotation snapshot</li>
	<li>centralized result directory construction</li>
</ul>

<p>These features make it easier to rerun analyses and trace outputs back to exact input settings.</p>

<h1>14. Caveats and Observations</h1>

<p>    1. Split values are used directly in file paths</p>

<p>If annotation values contain spaces or special characters, output paths may become inconvenient or unsafe.</p>

<ol start="2">
	<li>Silent ignoring of invalid split variables</li>
</ol>

<p>If a configured split_by key does not exist in annotation, it is ignored without warning.</p>

<ol start="3">
	<li>Intermediate outputs not enforced in rule all</li>
</ol>

<p>Many matrix outputs are commented out, so a successful workflow run may only guarantee final visualizations and export artifacts, not all intermediates.</p>

<ol start="4">
	<li>String-based enable/disable logic</li>
</ol>

<p>CQN and VOOM activation depend on empty-string checks, which is simple but brittle if config values are malformed.</p>

<ol start="5">
	<li>Integration naming assumes normalized method labels</li>
</ol>

<p>The workflow prepends norm during integration output generation, so config values in removeBatchEffect_parameters.norm_methods must align with that convention.</p>

<h1>15. Suggested Example Directory Tree</h1>

<ol start="15">
</ol>

<p>A typical output layout may look like this:</p>

<pre>
<code>results/spilterlize_integrate/
├── all/
│   └── plots/
│       ├── counts.png
│       ├── counts_heatmap_clustered.png
│       ├── filtered.png
│       ├── filtered_heatmap_clustered.png
│       ├── normCPM.png
│       ├── normCPM_heatmap_clustered.png
│       └── CFA.png
├── condition_control/
│   └── plots/
├── condition_treated/
│   └── plots/
├── envs/
│   ├── python_basics.yaml
│   ├── edgeR.yaml
│   ├── cqn.yaml
│   └── ggplot.yaml
└── configs/
├── myproject_config.yaml
└── myproject_annot.csv
</code></pre>

<h1>16. Recommended Documentation Header for Repository</h1>

<ol start="16">
</ol>

<p>You could place the following summary in a repository README:</p>

<p>spilterlize_integrate is a Snakemake workflow for split-wise expression data processing, normalization, optional batch integration, HVF transformation, and visualization. It derives analysis branches dynamically from project metadata and configuration, and produces per-split plots together with configuration and environment exports for reproducibility.</p>
]]></question>
