<h1 id="technical-documentation-genome_tracks-snakemake-workflow">Technical Documentation: genome_tracks Snakemake Workflow</h1>

<h1 id="1-purpose">1. Purpose</h1>

<p>This workflow defines a Snakemake module named genome_tracks for generating genome track visualizations and browser-compatible track assets from a set of genomic samples and target loci. The workflow reads project configuration, sample annotations, and a gene/region list, resolves target genomic intervals, and delegates processing to additional included rule files. Its final targets include per-gene track images, UCSC track hub metadata, exported Conda environments, and archived configuration files.</p>

<h1 id="2-workflow-overview">2. Workflow Overview</h1>

<p>Module identity</p>

<ul>
	<li>Module name: genome_tracks</li>
	<li>Minimum Snakemake version: 8.20.1</li>
	<li>Workflow report path: report/workflow.rst</li>
	<li>Primary config file: config/config.yaml</li>
</ul>

<p>Imported Python dependencies</p>

<p>The Snakefile imports:</p>

<ul>
	<li>pandas</li>
	<li>os</li>
	<li>gzip</li>
	<li>re</li>
	<li>numpy</li>
	<li>json</li>
	<li>hashlib</li>
	<li>min_version from snakemake.utils</li>
</ul>

<p>Not all imported libraries are directly used in the visible Snakefile fragment. Some may be required by included rule files or shared utility code. That is an inference based on the includes; it is not explicitly shown here.</p>

<h1 id="3-inputs">3. Inputs</h1>

<h2 id="31-configuration">3.1 Configuration</h2>

<p>The workflow expects a YAML configuration file at:</p>

<p>config/config.yaml</p>

<p>The visible Snakefile directly references the following configuration keys:</p>

<ul>
	<li>sample_annotation</li>
	<li>gene_list</li>
	<li>result_path</li>
	<li>file_type</li>
	<li>genome</li>
	<li>project_name</li>
	<li>optionally mem</li>
	<li>optionally threads</li>
</ul>

<p>Expected semantics of key config fields</p>

<p><u>Key: Purpose</u></p>

<ul>
	<li>sample_annotation: CSV file describing input samples and grouping metadata</li>
	<li>gene_list: CSV file defining target genes or genomic regions and visualization parameters</li>
	<li>result_path: Root output directory</li>
	<li>file_type: Output format for track images, such as png or pdf</li>
	<li>genome: Genome assembly identifier used in track hub path construction</li>
	<li>project_name: Prefix used for exported config and annotation snapshots</li>
	<li>mem: Memory assigned to rule all</li>
	<li>threads: Thread count assigned to rule all</li>
</ul>

<p>These meanings are derived from how the values are used in paths and rule parameters.</p>

<h2 id="32-sample-annotation-table">3.2 Sample annotation table</h2>

<p>The workflow loads the sample annotation sheet with:</p>

<p>annot = pd.read_csv(config['sample_annotation'])</p>

<p>This table must contain at least:</p>

<ul>
	<li>bam</li>
	<li>group</li>
</ul>

<p>group column behavior</p>

<p>The group field supports two modes:</p>

<ol>
	<li>Direct group label If group does not end with .tsv, it is treated as a regular visualization group label.</li>
	<li>Single-cell metadata file If group ends with .tsv, the file is interpreted as a per-cell metadata table. The workflow reads the second column ([1]) and extracts unique group values from it.</li>
</ol>

<p>This design allows the workflow to support both bulk/grouped samples and single-cell BAMs with group assignments stored externally.</p>

<h2 id="33-gene-list-table">3.3 Gene list table</h2>

<p>The workflow loads the gene list using:</p>

<p>gene_dict = pd.read_csv(config['gene_list'], index_col="gene_region").to_dict('index') genes = list(gene_dict.keys())</p>

<p>Therefore, the gene list CSV must contain:</p>

<ul>
	<li>a column named gene_region, used as the index</li>
	<li>a column named ymax, used during annotation assembly</li>
</ul>

<p>Supported gene_region values</p>

<p>Each gene_region entry can be either:</p>

<ul>
	<li>a genomic coordinate string matching chr[0-9XY]+:[0-9]+-[0-9]+</li>
	<li>a gene symbol or gene identifier resolvable by parse_gene()</li>
</ul>

<h1 id="4-internal-data-processing-logic">4. Internal Data Processing Logic</h1>

<h2 id="41-single-cell-group-extraction">4.1 Single-cell group extraction</h2>

<p>The workflow builds a dictionary of single-cell sample metadata keyed by an MD5 hash of the BAM path:</p>

<p>bam_hash = hashlib.md5(row['bam'].encode()).hexdigest()</p>

<p>Each entry stores:</p>

<ul>
	<li>the BAM path</li>
	<li>the metadata TSV path</li>
	<li>the unique group names extracted from column 2 of the TSV file</li>
</ul>

<p>Why hashing is used</p>

<p>The BAM path hash serves as a stable unique key for each single-cell sample. This avoids collisions that could happen if file basenames are reused. That rationale is inferred from the implementation comment and key structure.</p>

<p>Derived grouping variables</p>

<p>The workflow derives:</p>

<ul>
	<li>sc_groups: unique groups across all single-cell metadata files</li>
	<li>plot_groups: union of sc_groups and all non-TSV group labels from the annotation table, sorted lexicographically</li>
</ul>

<p>This means the workflow normalizes group definitions from two different sources into a single set of plotting groups.</p>

<h2 id="42-result-path-construction">4.2 Result path construction</h2>

<p>All module outputs are rooted under:</p>

<p>result_path = os.path.join(config["result_path"], module_name)</p>

<p>So the effective output directory is:</p>

<p><result_path_from_config><result_path_from_config>&lt;result_path_from_config&gt;/genome_tracks/</result_path_from_config></result_path_from_config></p>

<h2 id="43-target-generegion-resolution">4.3 Target gene/region resolution</h2>

<p>The workflow processes each requested <code>gene_region</code> entry as follows:</p>

<ol>
	<li>If the entry matches the genomic coordinate regex, it is parsed with <code>parse_region(...)</code>.</li>
	<li>Otherwise, it is resolved as a gene using <code>parse_gene(...)</code>.</li>
	<li>If <code>parse_gene(...)</code> returns <code>-1</code>, the gene is marked for removal.</li>
	<li>For successfully resolved entries, the result is combined with that row’s <code>ymax</code> value and appended to <code>gene_annot_list</code>.</li>
</ol>

<h3 id="important-dependency">Important dependency</h3>

<p>The functions <code>parse_region()</code> and <code>parse_gene()</code> are not defined in the visible Snakefile. They are likely provided by one of the included rule or helper files, most plausibly <code>rules/common.smk</code>, but that cannot be confirmed from the uploaded file alone.</p>

<h3 id="handling-unresolved-genes">Handling unresolved genes</h3>

<p>If one or more genes cannot be resolved:</p>

<ul>
	<li>the workflow creates the module result directory if needed</li>
	<li>writes them to: <code>genes_not_found.csv</code></li>
	<li>removes them from the active target list before downstream expansion</li>
</ul>

<p>This prevents failed symbol resolution from breaking all downstream outputs.</p>

<h2 id="44-normalized-gene-identifiers">4.4 Normalized gene identifiers</h2>

<p>After resolution, the workflow transforms gene labels by replacing <code>:</code> with <span style="color:#f1c40f;"><code>-</code>:</span></p>

<p>genes = [gene.replace(':', '-') for gene in genes]</p>

<p>This is likely done to make region-style identifiers safe for filenames.</p>

<p>The final resolved annotation table is then stored in memory as:</p>

<p>gene_annot_df = pd.DataFrame( gene_annot_list, columns=['chr', 'start', 'end', 'count', 'ymax'], index=genes )</p>

<h3 id="note-on-count">Note on <code>count</code></h3>

<p>The meaning of the <code>count</code> field is not defined in the visible Snakefile. It is presumably returned by <code>parse_region()</code> or <code>parse_gene()</code> and used by downstream visualization rules. That is an inference, not an explicit statement in the file.</p>

<h1 id="5-included-rule-files">5. Included Rule Files</h1>

<p>The Snakefile delegates implementation details to four included rule files:</p>

<ul>
	<li>include: os.path.join("rules", "common.smk")</li>
	<li>include: os.path.join("rules", "envs_export.smk")</li>
	<li>include: os.path.join("rules", "prepare.smk")</li>
	<li>include: os.path.join("rules", "visualization.smk")</li>
</ul>

<p>These likely provide:</p>

<ul>
	<li>shared parsing/helper functions</li>
	<li>environment export rules</li>
	<li>input preparation/transformation rules</li>
	<li>final track generation and visualization rules</li>
</ul>

<p>Because these files were not provided, the exact DAG beneath rule all cannot be documented at rule granularity from the current source alone.</p>

<h1 id="6-final-targets-rule-all">6. Final Targets (rule all)</h1>

<p>The workflow exposes a single aggregate target rule:</p>

<p>rule all:</p>

<p>Its inputs define the required deliverables for a successful run.</p>

<h2 id="61-genome-track-outputs">6.1 Genome track outputs</h2>

<p>Per-gene visualization files are expected at:</p>

<p><result_path><result_path>&lt;result_path&gt;/tracks/\{gene}.\{file_type}</result_path></result_path></p>

<p>expanded across all resolved genes.</p>

<h2 id="62-ucsc-track-hub-metadata">6.2 UCSC track hub metadata</h2>

<p>The workflow requires generation of:</p>

<p><result_path><result_path>&lt;result_path&gt;/bigWigs/&lt;genome&gt;<genome><genome>/trackDb.txt</genome></genome></result_path></result_path></p>

<p>This indicates support for publishing generated tracks through a UCSC Genome Browser track hub.</p>

<h2 id="63-exported-conda-environment-files">6.3 Exported Conda environment files</h2>

<p>The workflow expects YAML exports for these environments:</p>

<ul>
	<li>pygenometracks</li>
	<li>igv_reports</li>
	<li>sinto</li>
</ul>

<p>under:</p>

<p><result_path><result_path>&lt;result_path&gt;/envs/\{env}.yaml</result_path></result_path></p>

<h2 id="64-archived-configuration-snapshots">6.4 Archived configuration snapshots</h2>

<p>The workflow also materializes copies of key metadata into:</p>

<pre>
<code>
<result_path></result_path><result_path><result_path></result_path><result_path></result_path></result_path><result_path><result_path>&lt;result_path&gt;</result_path></result_path><result_path><result_path>/configs/gene_list.csv
<result_path></result_path></result_path><result_path></result_path><result_path><result_path></result_path><result_path></result_path><result_path><result_path></result_path></result_path></result_path></result_path><result_path><result_path><result_path><result_path>&lt;result_path&gt;</result_path></result_path></result_path></result_path><result_path><result_path><result_path><result_path>/configs/\{project_name}_config.yaml
<result_path></result_path></result_path></result_path><result_path></result_path><result_path><result_path></result_path><result_path></result_path><result_path><result_path><result_path></result_path></result_path></result_path></result_path></result_path></result_path><result_path><result_path><result_path><result_path><result_path><result_path>&lt;result_path&gt;</result_path></result_path></result_path></result_path></result_path></result_path><result_path><result_path><result_path><result_path><result_path><result_path>/configs/\{project_name}_annot.csv
</result_path></result_path></result_path></result_path></result_path></result_path></code></pre>

<p>These files support reproducibility by preserving the exact configuration and annotation inputs used for a run.</p>

<h2 id="65-temporarily-disabled-igv-report">6.5 Temporarily disabled IGV report</h2>

<p>An IGV report target exists in the Snakefile but is commented out:</p>

<p>igv_report = os.path.join(result_path, "igv-report.html"),</p>

<p>So IGV report generation appears to be planned or previously supported, but it is not currently part of the active target set.</p>

<h1 id="7-resources-and-logging">7. Resources and Logging</h1>

<p>rule all sets:</p>

<ul>
	<li>memory from config["mem"], defaulting to 4000</li>
	<li>threads from config["threads"], defaulting to 1</li>
	<li>log file at logs/rules/all.log</li>
</ul>

<p>Implication</p>

<p>These resource declarations apply to the aggregate rule and do not necessarily reflect the needs of the individual underlying rules. Actual heavy compute behavior is likely defined in the included rule files.</p>

